    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the difference between high glycemic index foods and low glycemic index foods? I'm trying to understand how they affect blood sugar and insulin response for someone managing type 2 diabetes.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Foods with a high glycemic index (GI) are digested and absorbed quickly, leading to rapid spikes in blood sugar and insulin levels. Examples include white bread and sugary drinks. Conversely, low GI foods are digested and absorbed more slowly, causing a gradual rise in blood sugar and a more moderate insulin response. Foods like whole grains and legumes have a low GI. For someone with type 2 diabetes, consuming low GI foods can help manage blood sugar levels and improve overall diabetes control.",
      "source": "target",
